  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nTERRAIN-BASED SHOWERS THURSDAY AND FRIDAY: \n\nThere will continue to be pockets of energy floating about Thursday \nand Friday which will contribute to isolated storm chances along the \nhigher terrain both days. In lower elevation areas, fairly mild\ndays will be on tap, with above normal temperatures expected \nthrough the end of the work week, before a pattern shift arrives\nthis weekend. \n\nPOTENTIAL FOR WIDESPREAD PRECIPITATION: \n\nThe aformentioned pattern shift leads to a much more complicated \nforecast with a lot of different variables at play. Firstly, a low \npressure system will begin to drop southward along the West Coast, \nwhich will begin to steer the tropical moisture from Hurricane \nRachel into the Desert Southwest by Saturday afternoon. This will \ntransition the flow aloft to southwesterly, which continues to be \nfavorable for moisture advection. Ensemble guidance is depicting \nPWATs upwards of 250% of normal for much of the region. This \nindicates the return of widespread precipitation chances for western \nColorado and eastern Utah this weekend, mostly favoring Sunday and \nMonday. There are uncertainties about how much cloud cover will be \npresent throughout the weekend, leading to questions regarding \nconvective or stratiform storm modes. It is worth waiting for HiRes \nguidance to help answer that question as we move closer to the \nweekend. \n\nThe low pressure system continues to spin over the Desert Southwest \nby the start of the work week, but PWATs begin to decrease from \nprevious days. This could indicate a break in widespread \nprecipitation chances, but even that seems questionable at this \ntime. Precipitation chances beyond Monday could also be dictated by \nTropical Storm Simon which is currently spinning off the southern \ncoast of Mexico and expected to make landfall as a hurricane. \nTherefore, beyond Monday, there are many unknowns. If there were to \nbe a second round of moisture as a result of Simon, it would likely \ntake place Tuesday onward, before the low pressure makes its exit \neastward over the Divide. At this far out, deterministic models for \neach wave of moisture should be taken with a grain of salt until we \nhave a better handle on the exact timing, intensity, and location of \nprecipitation chances for the coming week. All that being said, \nthere are still unknown details to this upcoming period, but we \nremain confident that early next week will see a return of more \nwidespread active weather. Stay tuned!\n\nLast thing to note for you snow lovers; as the temperatures begin to \ncool as a result of this low pressure system, we could see \nadditional accumulating snow on our highest mountain peaks, \nespecially those in the San Juans.",
